  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "A mechanical watch keeps time using stored spring energy instead of a battery. Its clever mechanism releases that energy in tiny, evenly timed steps. When you wind the watch, you tighten a mainspring inside a small drum called the barrel. The mainspring slowly unwinds, turning the barrel and feeding energy into a series of gears. This gear train carries rotation toward the watch hands while reducing the motion into useful speeds. But left alone, the spring would release its energy much too quickly. The escapement meters that energy, allowing the gears to advance one small step at a time. A balance wheel oscillates back and forth at a steady rate, telling the escapement when each step may happen. So the watch combines a steady oscillator, a controlled escapement and gears to turn spring energy into accurate time. Thank you.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9637803238195676,
